[00:20:32] after feature 1: 32 rulings; house rules for C: ['Across the business: whenever a figure has to be rounded, it is rounded up to the next whole unit (whole mg for doses, whole cent for money, whole 15-minute block for time), never to the nearest. A figure that is already whole stays as it is.', 'Across the business: a calculated amount is never allowed to go past its set ceiling. If it comes out above the ceiling, it is the ceiling (a dose over 500 mg is 500 mg).', 'Across the business: when a quantity used in a calculation (weight, dose per kg, minutes, hourly rate) is negative, missing, blank or unreadable, it counts as 0. The calculation then carries on with 0, and the result is a real 0 rather than blank or NULL. A negative amount is never treated as a refund or a credit.', 'Across the business: in size or price bands, a boundary value belongs to the lower band, because each band runs up to and including its upper limit (exactly 1 kg is micro, 6 is small, 25 is medium, 45 is large). A negative amount falls in the lowest band and is not treated as missing.', 'Across the business: when months are added to a date and the day does not exist in the target month, the extra days spill over into the following month. They are not clamped to the last day of the month (31 January plus 1 month is 3 March in 2026, and 29 February 2024 plus 12 months is 1 March 2025).', 'Across the business: dates are written in the exact form YYYY-MM-DD, with a two-digit month and day. Spaces around a date are ignored, but a month or day without a leading zero is not valid.', 'Across the business: names and codes that go into a tag are built the same way each time. Spaces at the start and end are removed, and only ASCII letters and digits are kept, so inner spaces, hyphens and accented letters are dropped. The result is in capitals, and a number is used as the text it is written as. A part that is NULL, empty or blank, or that ends up empty after cleaning, is replaced by STRAY. If the finished tag is longer than 12 characters, it is cut to its first 12.']
